A||||6||fierospeeder||BACK UP your DATA||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||04-29-2001||05:12 PM||||I just got my computer working again.  <P>I think it was friday.  I came home from school and wanted to log onto the fiero forum.<BR>I turn the puter on and as i come back i see an error message in windows.  I reboot the puter and as it is detecting all the hardware, then i get an error saying "cant find partition"<P>I tried norton, which recovered my partition but no hope, i tried fdisk which didn't work either.<P>I went to my uncles on saturday.  He let me borrow a harddrive and gave me two Fdisk utilities that he found on the internet.  The harddrive already had windows and the two utilities.  We hooked up the bad drive as a slave.  I came home and played with my computer.  Somehow i was able to recover my partitioned D and E drive, which had all my data that i wanted.  I wasn't able to recover C, but i think if i did use the utilities off a floppydrive i might have.  Because the original drive was bootable and the computer will only give the bootable drive that comes first.<BR>Just didn't really care what was on C.<P>I had this happen before but with using dblespace and 3 partitions.  I was able to find my files, cause' dblespace has the original files stored in one HUGE file.  This was on my IBM 486 170 meg.  Really pushing it.<P>Letting you guys know, backup your stuff, use your cdwriters.  It would suck to lose your fiero pics etc...  Ya i thought my puter was perfect, but it just crashed for no reason.  If anyone of you loses your partition, dont format your drive, or use Fdisk.  You can recover your data if your puter loses its partition data.  Also, harddrives are really cheap and running two of them is an excellent safeguard if you store your important stuff on both drives.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||SteveJ||04-29-2001||06:06 PM||||Never never never never never never use doublespace, Never never never, NO NO NO NO.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||04-29-2001||06:10 PM||||I'm sorry. I just see doublespace and I go nuts. I'm glad you got your data back. Using Western Digital drives? Could explain it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-29-2001||11:50 PM||||The only time I use the compression is on computers with small drives. It lets me have a place to install the applications and other things like that that will have to be rebuilt if the machine frys.<P><BR>For important data.... I just put it on my netware server with mirrored hard drives and a 6 tape autoloader. I can backup the server and all my local hard drives thanks to Backup Exec's workstation agents....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||04-30-2001||12:49 AM||||I only used that dblespace was when my harddrive was 170megs.  That was a long time ago.  <P>I dont think there should even be a use for it when harddrives are so large in storage space these days.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||04-30-2001||08:51 AM||||I agree. Always backup. Never use doublespace.  That pretty much covers it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Also, most people who try to build or work on their own computers don't realize just how fragile a HDD is.  If you set your Hard Drive on a hard table and can hear it touch, you've hit it hard enough to do damage.  Also, never - ever, move a HDD while it is running.  The head slap you get when you set your drive down too hard is in the park area of the disk, but the head slap you'll get if you move a running drive could be in data areas of the disk.  Instant data loss, and it's unrecoverable (pretty much).  Also, I know all those people who say "I've been working on my PC for years and I just touch the power supply to ground myself."  You're asking for trouble.  Get a wrist strap and use it.  PC companies wouldn't spend so much money on ESD control is static electricity wasn't a big danger to the hardware.  Ok, getting off the soap box now.  Just wanted to warn people.  I can't tell you how many times I've seen Network Engineers who should know better just toss a brand new $1000 SCSI drive onto their desk like a paperweight.  Other than static electricity, most everything is a PC is real durable, but not the HDD.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||JSocha||04-30-2001||10:46 AM||||&lt;stated with real dorky expression on face&gt;<P><B>"Backing up data <I>GOOOOOOOOOOOOODD!</I>"</B>||||reg||1
